course: english
lesson: 30
title: Relative clauses
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A relative clause is a clause that tells you more about a noun. «The boy who won the race smiled.» Which boy? The one who won the race. The relative clause «who won the race» hangs on the noun boy like a label on a present. It starts with a relative pronoun: who for people, which for things and animals, that for either, whose for belonging, where for places and when for times.
explain: Jack writes «The boy which lives next door.» Which is for things, so it should be «The boy who lives next door.» Gran in Yorkshire says, «That's the lass what helped me» (lass means girl). That is her dialect, the way people speak where she lives, and it isn't wrong at home; in standard English, for school writing, it is «the girl who helped me». Zara in London says, «My mate, whose mum is peng at cooking, made cake» (peng means really good): whose shows belonging, and it is not the same as who's, which means who is.
explain: Sometimes the relative clause is extra information, and then a pair of commas fences it off: «The dog, which was barking loudly, ran away.» Cover the part between the commas and the sentence still works. Commas can change the meaning: «My brother who lives in Leeds» suggests I have more than one brother and I mean the Leeds one; «My brother, who lives in Leeds,» suggests I have just one, and by the way he lives in Leeds.
explain: You can sometimes leave the relative pronoun out: «The book that I read was good» can be «The book I read was good.» But not when the pronoun is the one doing the action: «The boy that won smiled» can't lose that. And watch what the clause is attached to: in «The cat sat on the mat that was black», it is the mat that was black, not the cat. Ella says «The boss who's got rizz…» (rizz is slang for charm): a fine relative clause, but in writing say «charm».
fact: A relative clause gives more information about a noun.
fact: Relative clauses usually start with a relative pronoun: who, which, that, whose, where or when.
fact: Who is used for people, which for things and animals, and that for either.
fact: Whose shows belonging: «the girl whose bike was stolen».
fact: Who's is short for who is or who has, which is not the same as whose.
fact: Where can start a relative clause about a place: «the house where I live».
fact: A relative clause that adds extra information is separated by a pair of commas, or a comma and a full stop at the end.
fact: Commas around a relative clause can change the meaning of a sentence.
fact: The relative pronoun can often be left out when it is not the subject of the clause: «the book I read».
fact: A relative clause describes the noun it comes right after, so place it carefully.
fact: In some dialects people say «the lass what helped me»; in standard English it is «the girl who helped me».
fact: Slang: «lass» (Yorkshire, Scotland) means girl; «peng» (London) means really good; «rizz» means charm.
exercise: «The boy who won the race smiled.» What job does «who won the race» do? | It tells us which boy | It tells us when the race happened | It is the main clause | It tells us where the boy lived
exercise: Mia writes «The dog, which was barking loudly, ran away.» Why the commas? | They fence off extra information | They show the dog was in a list of animals | They show a question is coming | They mark where to stop and breathe in a poem
exercise: Jack writes «The boy which lives next door.» What should it be? | The boy who lives next door. | The boy what lives next door. | The boy whose lives next door. | The boy, lives next door.
exercise: Gran says «the lass what helped me». How is it said in standard English? | The girl who helped me | The girl which helped me | The girl whose helped me | The girl what helped me
exercise: «Add a relative clause: The car stopped.» Which answer does it? | The car, which was red, stopped. | The red car stopped suddenly. | The car stopped at the lights. | The car stopped because it was red.
exercise: Kai asks, «Can "where" be a relative pronoun?» What is the answer? | Yes: the house where I live | No, where only asks questions | No, only who and which can be | Yes, but only for people
exercise: Zara says «My mate, whose mum is peng at cooking, made cake.» What does whose show? | Belonging | Who is | A question | A place
exercise: Oliver asks, «Can I leave out "that" in "The book that I read was good"?» | Yes: «The book I read was good.» | No, never leave out «that» | Yes: «The book that read was good.» | Yes: «The book I was good.»
exercise: Ella says «The boss who's got rizz…» What word fits better in writing than rizz? | Charm | Rice | Rizzle | Rizzy
exercise: «The cat sat on the mat that was black.» What was black? | The mat | The cat | Both the cat and the mat | Neither
exercise: What does «peng» mean in London slang? | Really good | Very cold | Grumpy | A big friendly penguin
exercise: Which relative pronoun is used for things and animals? | which | who | whose | when
exercise: Which sentence uses «whose» correctly? | The girl whose bike was stolen cried. | The girl whose crying is my sister. | Whose the girl with the bike? | The girl, whose, cried.
exercise: Which is a relative clause? | who lives in Leeds | in the big house | very quickly | on Tuesday morning
exercise: What does «lass» mean in Yorkshire? | Girl | Last | Lazy | Lesson
test: «it's 'the boy which won', which is for everything innit». What's right? | Who is for people: «the boy who won» | Which is right for people and things | What is the standard word for people | Whose is the right word here, because the boy owns the race
test: ★★ Which sentence has a relative clause? | The teacher who helped me was kind. | The teacher helped me because I was stuck. | The kind teacher helped me. | After lunch, the teacher helped me.
test: ★★ «The dog which was barking loudly, ran away.» What is missing? | A comma after «dog» | A comma after «ran» | A full stop after «dog» | Nothing, it is correct
test: ★★★ «My brother who lives in Leeds is a chef» has no commas. What does that suggest? | I may have more than one brother, and I mean the one in Leeds | I have only one brother, and he happens to live in Leeds | My brother is not really a chef | Leeds is a person, not a place
test: ★★★ In which sentence can you leave out «that» and still be correct? | The cake that Mum made was huge. | The dog that bit me ran off. | The boy that won smiled. | The bus that comes at eight is late.
test: ★★★ «I met a girl at the park whose dog was huge.» Why is this sentence a bit muddled? | Whose seems to belong to the park, not the girl | It has no verb at all | Whose should be who's | Parks can't have relative clauses
test: In a cartoon, a pirate says «That's the parrot what stole me hat!» How would it be in standard English? | That's the parrot that stole my hat! | That's the parrot who's stole my hat! | That's the parrot what stole my hat! | That's the parrot whose stole my hat!
test: What would happen to «The boy who won the race smiled» if you took out the relative clause? | You'd still have a sentence, but not know which boy | It would no longer have a verb | It would become a question | Nothing, it says the same
test: «whose and who's are the same, just spelt different». Are they? | No, whose shows belonging; who's means who is | Yes, they mean the same thing | Yes, but who's is the American spelling | No, who's is the name of a small place in Wales
test: «that's the caff where we had chips». (caff is British slang for café.) What is «where we had chips»? | A relative clause about the caff | A fronted adverbial | A question | A main clause
exam: Identify the relative pronoun appropriate to the completion of «The girl ___ bicycle was stolen reported the theft». | whose | who's | which | what
exam: Which of the following sentences most accurately employs a relative clause in standard English? | The man who repaired the roof has departed. | The man what repaired the roof has departed. | The man which repaired the roof has departed. | The man whose repaired the roof has departed.
exam: Select the response consistent with the established conventions concerning the sentence «The dog, which was barking loudly, ran away». | The commas enclose additional information that may be omitted | The commas indicate a list | The commas render the sentence a question | The commas are superfluous and must be removed
exam: Ascertain, in the sentence «The cat sat on the mat that was black», which noun the relative clause describes. | The mat | The cat | Both nouns equally | No noun, the clause being adverbial
